
<|channel>thought

*   Topic: Digital photo backups.
*   Angle: A counterintuitive lesson from a hypothetical case.
*   Format: Substack Note.
*   Length: 60-100 words.
*   Style: Plain text, one or two short paragraphs, no hashtags, no title.

    *   Standard advice: Back up everything everywhere (3-2-1 rule).
    *   Counterintuitive twist: Over-backing up or digital hoarding leads to losing the *meaning* of memories because you never actually look at the photos.
    *   Hypothetical case: Someone with 50,000 photos across three cloud services who hasn't found a specific memory in years because they were too busy managing the "safety" of the files rather than the content.

    *   *Draft 1:* I have a friend who spent years perfecting a triple-redundant backup system. He was so worried about the folder structures and syncing hard drives that he forgot to actually start looking at the photos. Last week, he searched for a photo of his daughter's first birthday and realized he hadn't opened that specific folder in a decade. The lesson? Total safety without curation is just a digital graveyard. If you spend all your time protecting the data, you lose the memory. (88 words)

    *   *Word count check:* 88 words.
